1. Velocity: As blood viscosity increases, the velocity of blood flow generally decreases. This is because the more resistance the blood offers to flow, the slower it will move through the blood vessels. This effect is more pronounced in smaller blood vessels, where the cross-sectional area is smaller, and the resistance to flow is more pronounced.

2. Pressure: An increase in blood viscosity also leads to an increase in blood pressure. This is because the increased resistance of the blood requires the heart to exert more force to overcome the resistance and maintain blood flow. This can result in an increase in blood pressure, which can be detected in the form of a higher blood pressure reading.

In summary, an increase in blood viscosity can lead to a decrease in blood flow velocity and an increase in blood pressure. This can have several implications for overall health, as it can lead to an increased risk of atherosclerosis, stroke, and other cardiovascular conditions.
