","Lecithins are soluble in ordinary solvents except for **water**. Lecithins are amphipathic molecules, meaning they have both hydrophilic (water-loving) and hydrophobic (water-repelling) parts. The hydrophilic part is the phosphate group, and the hydrophobic part is the fatty acid chains. In water, the hydrophobic chains tend to aggregate, making the lecithin insoluble.  

Lecithins are soluble in organic solvents like alcohol, ether, and chloroform, where the hydrophobic chains can dissolve more easily.  

\boxed{\text{Lecithins are soluble in ordinary solvents except for water.}}
