"In the staging of oral cavity cancer, are radiographic findings alone sufficient to diagnose extranodal extension (ENE)?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
In the staging of oral cavity cancer, are radiographic findings alone sufficient to diagnose extranodal extension (ENE)?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The staging of oral cavity cancer is a complex process, and the presence of extranodal extension (ENE) is an important factor in determining the prognosis and treatment plan. Radiographic findings alone are not sufficient to diagnose ENE in the oral cavity. While imaging techniques such as CT scans and MRI scans can provide valuable information about the size and extent of the primary tumor and its relationship to surrounding structures, they may not be able to accurately identify the presence of microscopic ENE.

To confirm the presence of ENE, a physical examination by a healthcare professional, including palitation of the tumor and surrounding tissues, is necessary. In some cases, a biopsy of the tumor and surrounding tissues may also be required to confirm the presence of tumor cells in the perineural stroma or other distant areas.

In summary, while radiographic findings can provide valuable information about the potential for EENE, they should not be used alone to diagnose ENE. A combination of physical examination, imaging, and if necessary, biopsy is necessary for accurate diagnosis.
