","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔS = ΣS(products) - ΣS(reactants)

For the reaction:

3 CO(g) + 3/2 O2(g) → 3 CO2(g)

We need to find the standard molar entropy (S°) for each substance involved. The values can be found in a standard thermodynamic table.

S°(CO) = 186.3 J/mol·K
S°(O2) = 205.0 J/mol·K
S°(CO2) = 213.8 J/mol·K

Now, we can plug these values into the equation:

ΔS = [3 * S°(CO2)] - [3 * S°(CO) + 3/2 * S°(O2)]

ΔS = [3 * 213.8 J/mol·K] - [3 * 186.3 J/mol·K + 3/2 * 205.0 J/mol·K]

ΔS = [641.4 J/mol·K] - [558.9 J/mol·K + 307.5 J/mol·K]

ΔS = 641.4 J/mol·K - 866.4 J/mol·K

ΔS = -225.0 J/mol·K

The change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction is -225.0 J/mol·K.
