","In a double-stranded DNA molecule, the bases pair up as follows: adenine (A) pairs with thymine (T), and guanine (G) pairs with cytosine (C). Therefore, the number of each base in the double-stranded DNA molecule will be equal to the complementary base count.

- Adenine (A): 20 (since A pairs with T)
- Thymine (T): 22 (since T pairs with A)
- Guanine (G): 25 (since G pairs with C)
- Cytosine (C): 30 (since C pairs with G)

So, the complete double-stranded DNA molecule contains 20 adenines, 22 thymines, 25 guanines, and 30 cytosines.
