- The coefficient of friction (\(\mu\)) is a ratio of the frictional force to the normal force.  
- Frictional force is given by \(F = \mu N\), where \(N\) is the normal force.  
- The units of frictional force are \(MLT^{-2}\) (mass × acceleration, derived from \(MLT^{-2}\)).  
- The units of normal force are also \(MLT^{-2}\).  
- When dividing the units of frictional force by the units of normal force, the units cancel out, leaving no units for the coefficient of friction.  

Thus, the dimensional representation is \([M^0L^0T^0]\).
