What is the change in equilibrium concentration of a complex ion (FeCN$_6^{4-}$) in a 1.0 M iron (III) chloride solution when 0.25 moles of potassium cyanide (KCN) is added? The K$_{eq}$ value for the formation of FeCN$_6^{4-}$ is 1.2 x 10$^{20}$.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To solve this problem, we need to consider the equilibrium reaction and the changes in concentration due to the addition of KCN.

The formation of the complex ion FeCN$_6^{4-}$ can be represented by the following equilibrium reaction:

Fe$^{3+} + 6CN^- \rightleftharpoons Fe(CN)_6^{4-}$

Let's denote the initial concentration of Fe$^{3+}$ as [Fe$^{3+}]_0$ and the initial concentration of CN$^-$ as [CN$^-]_0$. Since the initial concentration of Fe(CN)$_6^{4-}$ is 0, we can set up the equilibrium expression:

$$K_{eq} = \frac{[\text{Fe(CN)}_6^{4-}]}{[\text{Fe}^{3+}][\text{CN}^-]^6}$$

Given that the initial concentration of Fe$^{3+}$ is 1.0 M and the initial concentration of CN$^-$ is 0.25 M (from the addition of 0.25 moles of KCN in 1 L of solution), we can plug these values into the equilibrium expression:

$$1.2 \times 10^{20} = \frac{[\text{Fe(CN)}_6^{4-}]}{(1.0 - x)(0.25 - 6x)^6}$$

Since the equilibrium constant is very large, the reaction will shift almost completely to the right, forming almost all of the FeCN$_6^{4-}$. Therefore, we can assume that $x$ is very small compared to 1.0 and 0.25. This allows us to approximate the equation as:

$$1.2 \times 10^{20} \approx \frac{[\text{Fe(CN)}_6^{4-}]}{(1.0)(0.25)^6}$$

Solving for $[\text{Fe(CN)}_6^{4-}]$:

$$[\text{Fe(CN)}_6^{4-} ] \approx 1.2 \times 10^{20} \times (0.25)^6$$

Calculating the value:

$$[\text{Fe(CN)}_6^{4-} ] \approx 1.2 \times 10^{20} \times 6.25 \times 10^{-6} \approx 7.5 \times 10^{14} \text{ M}$$

So, the change in equilibrium concentration of FeCN$_6^{4-}$ is approximately $7.5 \times 10^{14}$ M.
