A jogger running at 9 km/hr alongside a railway track is 150 m ahead of the engine of a train running at 45 km/hr in the same direction. The train takes 25 seconds to pass the jogger. How long is the train in meters?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","1. **Convert the engine speed to meters per second**:  
   \[
   45 \text{ km/hr} \times \frac{1000 \text{ m}}{1 \text{ km}} \times \frac{1 \text{ hr}}{3600 \text{ s}} = 12.5 \text{ m/s}
   \]

2. **Calculate the relative speed of the train relative to the jogger**:  
   \[
   12.5 \text{ m/s} - 9 \text{ m/s} = 2.5 \text{ m/s}
   \]

3. **Determine the length of the train**:  
   \[
   \text{Length} = \text{Relative speed} \times \text{Time} = 2.5 \text{ m/s} \times 25 \text{ s} = 62.5 \text{ m}
   \]

   However, the jogger is 150 m ahead of the engine, so the total length of the train is:  
   \[
   62.5 \text{ m} + 150 \text{ m} = 212.5 \text{ m}
   \]

   Therefore, the length of the train is \(\boxed{212.5 \text{ m}}\).
