","No, the statement is not true. The condition \(\alpha = \beth_{\alpha}\) is not sufficient to guarantee \(\left|L_{\alpha}\right| = \left|V_{\alpha}\right|\), and the converse is also not true. 

1. **Why \(\alpha = \beth_{\alpha}\) does not guarantee \(\left|L_{\alpha}\right| = \left|V_{\alpha}\right\)**:
   - The \(\beth_{\alpha}\) is the \(\alpha\)-th Bregner number, defined as the number of subsets of \(\left\{ V_{\alpha} \right\}\).
   - The \(\left|L_{\alpha}\right|\) is the number of elements in the \(\alpha\)-th level of the lower hull of the universe, \(\{ L_{\alpha} \cap V_{\alpha} \mid \alpha \in I \}\).
   - The \(\left|V_{\alpha}\right|\) is the number of elements in the \(\alpha\)-th level of the universe.
   - The \(\beth_{\alpha}\) is defined in terms of the universe, while \(\left|L_{\alpha}\right|\) is defined in terms of the lower hull of the universe. The lower hull is a subset of the universe, so \(\left|L_{\alpha}\right|\) is always less than or equal to \(\left|V_{\alpha}\right|\).
   - For example, \(\left|L_{\alpha}\right| = \left|V_{\alpha}\right\}\) holds for \(\alpha = 1\) and \(\alpha = 2\), but \(\left|L_{\alpha}\right| < \left|V_{\alpha}\right\}\) holds for \(\alpha = 3\).

