   For an isotropic material, the principal normal strains are found by solving the characteristic equation:
   \[
   \begin{vmatrix}
   \sigma_x - \lambda & \tau_{xy} \\
   \tau_{xy} & \sigma_y - \lambda
   \end{vmatrix} = 0
   \]
   Substituting the given strains:
   \[
   \begin{vmatrix}
   \sigma_x - \lambda & \tau_{xy} \\
   \tau_{xy} & \sigma_y - \lambda
   \end{vmatrix} = 0 \implies (\sigma_x - \lambda)(\sigma_y - \lambda) - \tau_{xy}^2 = 0
   \]
   For an isotropic material, the stress-strain relationship is:
   \[
   \sigma_x = E \varepsilon_x, \quad \sigma_y = E \varepsilon_y, \quad \tau_{xy} = 2G \gamma_{xy}
   \]
   Substituting these into the characteristic equation:
   \[
   E \varepsilon_x - \lambda)(E \varepsilon_y - \lambda) - (2G \gamma_{xy})^2 = 0
   \]
