A triangular-shaped cantilever beam of uniform thickness \( t \), width \( b \) at the fixed end, and length \( L \) is subjected to a concentrated load \( P \) at the free end. The Young’s modulus of the material is \( E \). What is the maximum deflection of the beam?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The maximum deflection of a triangular-shaped cantilever beam with a concentrated load at the free end is derived as follows:

1. **Derivation**:
   The maximum deflection occurs at the free end of the beam. The deflection \( y \) at any point along the beam is given by:
   \[
   y = \frac{P}{E} \int_0^x \frac{x}{t} \left( \frac{L - x}{L} \right)^2 \, dx
   \]
   Substituting \( x = L \) to find the maximum deflection \( y_{\text{max}} \):
   \[
   y_{\text{max}} = \frac{P}{E} \int_0^L \frac{x}{t} \left( \frac{L - x}{L} \right)^2 \, dx
   \]
