What is the magnetic field strength at the center of a circular coil with 100 turns that carries a current of 1 A and has a radius of 0.2 m?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The magnetic field strength at the center of a circular coil is given by the formula:
\[
B = \frac{\mu_0 n I}{2R}
\]
where:
- \( B \) is the magnetic field strength,
- \(\mu_0\) is the permeability of free space (\(4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T m/A}\)),
- \( n \) is the number of turns in the coil,
- \( I \) is the current flowing through the coil,
- \( R \) is the radius of the coil.

Substituting the given values:
\[
B = \frac{(4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T m/A}) \times 100 \times 1 \, \text{A}}{2 \times 0.2 \, \text{m}}
\]
\[
B = \frac{4\pi \times 10^{-7} \times 100}{0.4}
\]
\[
B = \pi \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T}
\]
\[
B = 3.14 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T}
\]

Thus, the magnetic field strength at the center of the coil is \(\boxed{3.14 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T}}\).
