What is the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a photon scattered by an electron is given by the Compton effect formula:

\[
\Delta \lambda = \frac{2 \pi \hbar}{m_e c} (1 - \cos \theta)
\]

where:
- \(\Delta \lambda\) is the change in wavelength,
- \(\hbar\) is the reduced Planck constant (\(1.054 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s}\)),
- \(m_e\) is the electron mass (\(9.109 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}\)),
- \(c\) is the speed of light (\(2.998 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}\)),
- \(\theta\) is the scattering angle.

For the maximum change in wavelength, \(\theta = 180^\circ\), so \(\cos \theta = -1\). Substituting the values:

\[
\Delta \lambda = \frac{2 \pi (1.054 \times 10^{-34})}{9.109 \times 10^{-31} \times 2.998 \times 10^8} (1 + 1) = 4.27 \times 10^{-12} \, \text{m} = 4.27 \, \text{pm}
\]

The percentage change is:

\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{\Delta \lambda}{\lambda_0} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{4.27 \times 10^{-12}}{2.00 \times 10^{-10}} \right) \times 100 = 21.35\%
\]

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength is \(\boxed{21.35\%}\).
