","To solve this problem, we analyze the equilibrium condition of the piston. The piston remains stationary because the forces acting on it are balanced. The forces acting on the piston are:

1. The pressure in cylinder A (P_A) acting on the piston.
2. The pressure in cylinder B (P_B) acting on the piston.
3. The weight of the piston (W).

The net force is:
\[
P_A A_A - P_B A_B - W = 0
\]
where:
- \( A_A = \frac{\pi}{4} (100 \text{ mm})^2 = 7854 \text{ mm}^2 = 7.854 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}^2 \)
- \( A_B = \frac{\pi}{4} (25 \text{ mm})^2 = 196.3 \text{ mm}^2 = 1.963 \times 10^{-4} \text{ m}^2 \)
- \( W = m g = 25 \text{ kg} \times 9.807 \text{ m/s}^2 = 245.175 \text{ N} \)

Substituting the values:
\[
500 \times 10^3 \text{ Pa} \times 7.854 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}^2 - P_B \times 1.963 \times 10^{-4} \text{ m}^2 - 245.175 \text{ N} = 0
\]
Solving for \( P_B \):
\[
P_B = \frac{500 \times 10^3 \text{ Pa} \times 7.854 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}^2 - 245.175 \text{ N}}{1.963 \times 10^{-4} \text{ m}^2}
\]
\[
P_B = 196,300 \text{ Pa} = 196.3 \text{ kPa}
\]
Thus, the gas pressure in cylinder B is \(\boxed{196.3 \text{ kPa}}\).
